          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Capacity vs Weight</h3>
          <p>
            Read the table from the top down and a pattern jumps out. Capacity roughly doubles at each step, while weight climbs even faster. A RIVER 3 Plus is a one-hand carry; a DELTA 3 is a two-hand carry; a DELTA 2 Max is a 'pick a spot and leave it there' unit; and the DELTA Pro models are wheeled appliances. Decide how often you will physically move the station before you decide how much capacity you want, because the most common regret we hear is from owners who bought a 2kWh unit for camping and never take it out of the garage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Solar Input</h3>
          <p>
            The second column to watch is solar input. A station can only refill as fast as its charge controller allows, so a DELTA 3 Classic with a single 500W input will never benefit from a third or fourth panel, while a DELTA Pro 3 can use a full 2,600W array. If you plan to run off solar for multiple days, solar input matters as much as battery size. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-charge-a-portable-power-station-with-solar/">charging a power station with solar</a> walks through matching panels to these limits.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Expandability</h3>
          <p>
            Finally, look at expandability as an insurance policy. The price gap between the DELTA 3 Classic and the DELTA 3 Plus is essentially what you pay to keep the option of doubling capacity later. If you are buying for a growing household or a home that loses power more than once a year, that option tends to pay for itself. If you are buying a single camping battery, it usually does not.
          </p>
