        <div id="river-vs-delta" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. RIVER vs DELTA vs DELTA Pro Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            EcoFlow organizes its lineup into three families, and once you know what each family is for, picking a model gets much easier. The RIVER line covers small, carryable stations under about 1kWh. The DELTA line covers the 1kWh to 2kWh middle, where most home-backup and serious camping buyers land. The DELTA Pro line covers whole-home and near-whole-home backup, with 120/240V output and capacity measured in many kilowatt-hours.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">RIVER: charge things, run small things</h3>

          <p>
            RIVER models are for phones, laptops, cameras, lights, fans, CPAP machines and networking gear. With 245Wh to 286Wh on board and 300W to 600W of output, they are best understood as very large power banks with AC outlets. They are excellent for camping, road trips, home offices and as a short-outage router battery. They are the wrong tool for refrigerators, microwaves, coffee makers or anything with a heating element running more than a few minutes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">DELTA: the sweet spot for most households</h3>

          <p>
            DELTA models start at 1,024Wh with 1,800W output and go up to 2,048Wh with 2,400W in the DELTA 2 Max class. That is enough to keep a refrigerator, lights, internet and phones going through an overnight outage, run a microwave or coffee maker briefly, and handle most corded power tools. Weight runs from the mid-20s to about 50 pounds. For most readers of our <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">best portable power station guide</a>, a DELTA is the right answer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">DELTA Pro: backing up the house itself</h3>

          <p>
            DELTA Pro models are built to connect to your home's electrical system. They offer 240V output for well pumps and larger appliances, accept multi-kilowatt solar arrays, and integrate with EcoFlow's Smart Home Panel 2 for automatic switchover. They also weigh 100 pounds or more. Buy a DELTA Pro if you want a battery to take over specific household circuits when the grid drops. If you just want a big battery to plug a fridge into with an extension cord, a DELTA with an extra battery is cheaper and easier to move.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="older-models" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Older EcoFlow Models: Should You Buy Them on Sale?
          </h2>

          <p>
            EcoFlow keeps older models on the market long after their replacements launch, and retailers discount them heavily. Some of those deals are genuinely good. Others are clearance stock that only looks cheap until you compare it with a current model on sale. Here is how we would think about each one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">DELTA 2: yes, at the right price</h3>

          <p>
            The DELTA 2 carries 1,024Wh of LiFePO4 storage, 1,800W of output and 500W of solar input, and it expands to about 3kWh with an extra battery. On paper that is close to a DELTA 3 Classic, with slower AC charging and an older app generation. If you find a DELTA 2 for well under the current price of a DELTA 3 Classic, buy it. If the gap is small, buy the newer unit, since it will receive firmware support longer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Original DELTA Pro: only at a deep discount</h3>

          <p>
            The first-generation DELTA Pro is a 3,600Wh unit with 3,600W of output, around 99 pounds, and up to 1,600W of solar input. It was a landmark product, but it uses NCM lithium-ion cells rather than LiFePO4, which means a lower rated cycle life and more caution about heat. Our <a href="/lifepo4-vs-lithium-ion-power-stations/">LiFePO4 vs lithium-ion guide</a> explains the difference. The DELTA Pro still works with the Smart Home Panel ecosystem and can be a smart buy for occasional outage use, but only when it costs substantially less than a DELTA Pro 3. For daily solar cycling, pay for the newer chemistry.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">RIVER 2, RIVER 2 Max and RIVER 2 Pro</h3>

          <p>
            The RIVER 2 family was EcoFlow's first all-LiFePO4 small lineup, and all three are still solid. The RIVER 2 has 256Wh and 300W, the RIVER 2 Max has 512Wh and 500W, and the RIVER 2 Pro has 768Wh and 800W. The Max and Pro are interesting because they fill a capacity gap in the current lineup between the RIVER 3 Plus and the DELTA 3. If you want about 500Wh to 800Wh in a single box under 20 pounds and you find a RIVER 2 Max or RIVER 2 Pro on sale, it is a sensible buy. The base RIVER 2 is only worth it if it is cheaper than a discounted RIVER 3.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Rule for Older Models</h3>
          <p>
            The rule of thumb across all of these: an older LiFePO4 EcoFlow on a real discount is a good deal; an older NCM unit at near-full price is not. Check the chemistry, check the current-model sale price, and only buy the older unit if it saves meaningful money.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="solar-generator-kits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. EcoFlow Solar Generator Kits
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What a Solar Generator Kit Is</h3>
          <p>
            When EcoFlow or a retailer sells an 'EcoFlow solar generator,' it is a power station bundled with one or more solar panels. There is no separate hardware inside. The bundles can save money compared with buying the pieces separately, but only if the panel size matches what you will actually use. Our <a href="/best-solar-generator/">best solar generator roundup</a> covers kits from other brands too.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Matching Panels to the Station</h3>
          <p>
            Matching panels to the station is the whole game. A RIVER 3 pairs naturally with a single 100W to 110W panel. A RIVER 3 Plus works best with one 200W to 220W panel. A DELTA 3 Classic is limited to about 500W, so two 220W panels is the sensible ceiling. The DELTA 3 Plus and DELTA 2 Max can take two separate panel strings, and the DELTA Pro 3 can take a much larger array split between its high- and low-voltage inputs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Real-World Panel Output</h3>
          <p>
            Expect real-world panel output well below the label. A 220W panel in good sun at a decent angle commonly produces something like 140W to 170W for the middle of the day, and much less in winter, morning or under clouds. That means a 1,024Wh DELTA 3 with one 220W panel takes most of a sunny day to refill from empty. Two panels roughly halves that. If solar is your primary charging source rather than a backup, size the array to at least half the battery's capacity in watts.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Third-Party Panels</h3>
          <p>
            EcoFlow's rigid and bifacial panels are good, but they are not required. Any panel whose open-circuit voltage and current fall within the station's input range will work, and third-party panels from our <a href="/best-portable-solar-panel/">best portable solar panel guide</a> are often cheaper per watt. The bundle is worth buying when its discount beats what you would pay for a comparable third-party panel. Otherwise, buy the station alone and choose your panels separately.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="home-backup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Smart Home Panel and EcoFlow Home Backup
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Extension Cords vs Home Wiring</h3>
          <p>
            Plugging a refrigerator into a power station with an extension cord works, and for short outages it is all most people need. But if you want your home's circuits to switch over automatically when the grid drops, you need a transfer device between the station and your electrical panel. EcoFlow offers two main paths.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Smart Home Panel 2</h3>
          <p>
            The first is the Smart Home Panel 2, EcoFlow's own transfer panel. It installs next to your main breaker panel, takes over a selection of circuits you choose, and switches them to battery power automatically during an outage. It works with the DELTA Pro 3, DELTA Pro Ultra and original DELTA Pro, and it can manage multiple stations at once. Through the app you can see which circuits are drawing power and shed loads you do not need. Installation requires a licensed electrician, and the panel plus installation is a meaningful cost on top of the battery.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Transfer Switch or Interlock</h3>
          <p>
            The second path is a conventional transfer switch or interlock with a power inlet box, the same hardware used for portable generators. This works with any station that has a high-output AC port, including the DELTA 2 Max and DELTA 3 Plus, though 120V-only models can only feed one leg of your panel. It is cheaper than the Smart Home Panel but manual: you flip the switch yourself when the power goes out. Our <a href="/generator-transfer-switch-types-cost-and-installation/">guide to transfer switch types and costs</a> compares the options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which Path Makes Sense</h3>
          <p>
            Which path makes sense depends on how often you lose power and what you need to run. If outages are rare and short, skip the wiring and keep an extension cord ready. If outages are frequent, last for days, or you need to run a 240V well pump, the DELTA Pro 3 with the Smart Home Panel 2 is the cleanest EcoFlow setup. If you are comparing EcoFlow against installed batteries, our <a href="/best-home-battery-backup-system/">home battery backup system guide</a> covers wall-mounted alternatives.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ecoflow-app" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. The EcoFlow App and Ecosystem
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Monitoring</h3>
          <p>
            Every current EcoFlow station connects to the EcoFlow app over Wi-Fi, Bluetooth or both. The app shows battery level, input and output wattage, estimated time remaining and the status of each port, and it lets you switch outlets on and off remotely. That alone is useful during an outage, when you want to check the battery from upstairs without walking to the garage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Charging and Storage Settings</h3>
          <p>
            The more practical features are the settings. You can cap the AC charging rate so the station charges slowly and quietly overnight, set charge and discharge limits to protect battery health during storage, and configure how long the station waits before shutting down idle outputs. For a station that lives on a shelf as backup, setting the charge limit to about 80% for long-term storage and topping off before storm season is a simple way to extend battery life. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-do-portable-power-stations-last/">how long portable power stations last</a> covers storage habits in more detail.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ecosystem and Firmware</h3>
          <p>
            The app is also where the ecosystem lives. Smart Home Panel scheduling, extra-battery status, solar input history and firmware updates all run through it. EcoFlow pushes firmware updates that can change charging behavior and fix bugs, so it is worth connecting at least occasionally even if you prefer to use the station offline. Owners commonly report that the app is among the more polished in the category, though Wi-Fi pairing can be fiddly on some home networks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Using It Without the App</h3>
          <p>
            You do not need the app to use an EcoFlow. Every station works from its front-panel buttons and screen. But if you plan to use solar, expand capacity or wire into your home, the app turns the station from a battery with outlets into a system you can actually manage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Which EcoFlow Size Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Add Up Your Loads</h3>
          <p>
            The fastest way to choose a size is to add up what you want to run, estimate the hours, and compare the total with each station's usable capacity. Plan on roughly 85% of the rated capacity being available after inverter losses. Our <a href="/what-size-portable-power-station-do-i-need/">power station sizing guide</a> has a full worksheet; the quick version is below.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Rated Capacity, Estimated Fridge Runtime at 120W Average</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~2 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">RIVER 3 Plus (286Wh)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~7 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">DELTA 3 Classic / Plus (1,024Wh)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~14 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">DELTA 2 Max (2,048Wh)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~29 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">DELTA Pro 3 (4,096Wh)</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Refrigerator Runtime</h3>
          <p>
            Those numbers come from simple arithmetic, not lab tests: usable watt-hours divided by average draw. A modern full-size refrigerator typically averages 100W to 150W over a day, even though its compressor may pull several hundred watts while running. Our article on whether <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-refrigerator/">a power station can run a refrigerator</a> goes into startup surge and duty cycle.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">CPAP Runtime</h3>
          <p>
            For a CPAP, the math is friendlier. A machine without humidifier often draws 30W to 60W, so a RIVER 3 Plus covers a night and a DELTA 3 covers several. Heated humidifiers can double or triple the draw, which is where the extra capacity of a DELTA pays off. See our guide on <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-cpap/">running a CPAP from a power station</a> for model-specific notes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Three Practical Choices</h3>
          <p>
            For most households the decision collapses to three options. Pick a RIVER 3 or RIVER 3 Plus if you need portability and only charge devices or run small electronics. Pick a DELTA 3 Plus or DELTA 2 Max if you want to cover a fridge, lights and internet through an overnight outage. Pick a DELTA Pro 3 if you want hours to days of backup for several circuits, or need 240V.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ecoflow-vs-competitors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. EcoFlow vs Jackery, Bluetti and Anker
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where EcoFlow Wins</h3>
          <p>
            EcoFlow's main advantages over competitors are charging speed, app polish and the breadth of its home-backup ecosystem. Its fast AC charging has been a selling point for years, and the Smart Home Panel 2 gives the DELTA Pro line a more integrated home-backup path than most rivals offer. Its weaknesses are price, frequent model churn that makes the lineup confusing, and a tendency for older models to fall off the support radar sooner than buyers expect.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Jackery</h3>
          <p>
            Jackery is EcoFlow's most direct rival for casual buyers. Its Explorer line tends to be simpler, with fewer settings and a reputation for straightforward reliability. Our head-to-head <a href="/jackery-vs-ecoflow/">Jackery vs EcoFlow comparison</a> breaks down which brand wins at each size. In short: Jackery for simplicity, EcoFlow for faster charging and expansion.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bluetti</h3>
          <p>
            Bluetti competes hard on capacity per dollar and on modular systems with large expansion batteries. If you want the most watt-hours for the money and do not care about the app, Bluetti often comes out ahead. Our <a href="/bluetti-vs-ecoflow/">Bluetti vs EcoFlow breakdown</a> covers the tradeoffs, including where Bluetti's charging speeds and build quality trail EcoFlow's.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Anker SOLIX</h3>
          <p>
            Anker's SOLIX line has become a serious contender in the 1kWh and 3kWh-plus classes, with long warranties and strong home-backup models. If you are cross-shopping, compare warranty length, real AC charging speed and the cost of extra batteries rather than headline capacity alone. Any of these brands beats a gas generator for indoor-safe, quiet backup, as our <a href="/portable-power-station-vs-generator/">power station vs generator guide</a> explains, but none of them replaces a generator for long outages without solar.
          </p>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best EcoFlow power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, the DELTA 3 Plus. It has 1,024Wh of LiFePO4 storage, an 1,800W inverter, fast AC charging and the option to add extra batteries later. Choose the DELTA Pro 3 if you want to back up house circuits with 240V output, or the RIVER 3 Plus if you need something you can carry one-handed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is EcoFlow a good brand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. EcoFlow is one of the largest portable power station brands, its current lineup uses long-life LiFePO4 batteries, and it is known for fast charging and a capable app. The main complaints are price and frequent model changes that make the lineup confusing. Buy current models when you can, and check warranty terms before buying older stock.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between the DELTA 3 Plus and DELTA 3 Classic?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both have 1,024Wh of capacity and 1,800W output. The DELTA 3 Plus adds expansion with extra batteries, two solar inputs and generally faster charging hardware. The Classic has one 500W solar input and EcoFlow does not list extra-battery support. If you might want more capacity later, buy the Plus; if not, the Classic saves money.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can an EcoFlow power my whole house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A DELTA Pro Ultra with multiple batteries and the Smart Home Panel 2 can cover most of a typical home, including 240V loads. A DELTA Pro 3 covers a selection of essential circuits. Smaller DELTA and RIVER models cannot power a whole house, but a DELTA can keep a refrigerator, lights and internet running through an overnight outage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is an EcoFlow solar generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is an EcoFlow power station sold in a bundle with one or more solar panels. The station stores the energy and the panels recharge it. Bundles can save money, but make sure the panel wattage matches the station's solar input limit. You can also buy the station alone and pair it with compatible third-party panels.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy an older EcoFlow like the DELTA 2 or DELTA Pro?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The DELTA 2 is a good buy if it is clearly cheaper than a DELTA 3 Classic, since it uses the same LiFePO4 chemistry. The original DELTA Pro uses NCM cells with a shorter rated cycle life, so only buy it at a steep discount for occasional outage use, not for daily solar cycling.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does an EcoFlow battery last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Current EcoFlow models use LiFePO4 cells rated for thousands of full charge cycles before falling to about 80% of original capacity. For typical backup and camping use, that means many years of service. Storing the station at a partial charge, keeping it out of extreme heat and cycling it every few months all help extend its life.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a RIVER for refrigerator backup</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A RIVER 3 or RIVER 3 Plus holds under 300Wh, which runs a typical fridge for only two or three hours. If keeping food cold through an overnight outage is the goal, start with a 1kWh DELTA. RIVER models are for charging devices and running small electronics, not kitchen appliances.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for a DELTA Pro you will never wire in</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The DELTA Pro 3 shines with a transfer panel, solar and 240V loads. If it will sit in a closet and run a fridge on an extension cord, a DELTA 3 Plus with an extra battery delivers similar capacity for far less money and is much easier to move around.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overbuying solar panels for a small input</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every EcoFlow has a maximum solar input, and panels beyond that limit add nothing. A DELTA 3 Classic tops out around 500W, so a 1,000W array wastes half its output. Check the input limit and voltage range before you buy panels, or the extra wattage is money spent on nothing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying older NCM stock at near-full price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The original DELTA Pro and older non-LFP models use NCM chemistry with a shorter rated cycle life. They can still be good deals at deep discounts, but paying close to current-model prices for older chemistry is a poor trade when a LiFePO4 replacement costs about the same.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing it at 100% and forgetting it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A power station left fully charged in a hot garage for a year loses capacity faster than one stored at partial charge in a cool room. Use the app to set a storage charge limit, check the battery every few months, and top it off before storm season starts.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

